 ## Refutation
 
 1.1 Put $x=\pi/4$, $s=\sin x$, and $c=\cos x$. Since $0<\pi/4<\pi/2$ by [F4], [F3] gives $s,c>0$. By [F7] and [F5], $0=\cos(\pi/2)=\cos(2x)=c^2-s^2$, so $(c-s)(c+s)=0$. Since $c+s>0$, we have $c=s$. Then [F7] and [F5] give $1=\sin(\pi/2)=\sin(2x)=2sc=2s^2$, so [F9] gives $s=1/\sqrt2$. Thus $\arcsin(1/\sqrt2)=\pi/4$ by [F2], and $k_*=\pi/(4(\pi/4))-1/2=1/2$. [given, F2, F3, F4, F5, F7, F9, algebra]
 
-2.1 The legal standard query counts are integers, so $k_*=1/2$ is not executable. Applying the integer formula [F1] gives $p_0=\sin^2(\pi/4)=1/2$ and $p_1=\sin^2(3\pi/4)=1/2$, whereas substituting the noninteger $k_*$ into the trigonometric expression would give $\sin^2(\pi/2)=1$. The continuous expression therefore does not describe an allowed standard Grover circuit at this input. [given, F1, F5, step 1.1, algebra] ∎
